---
title: "Animated ticks and cross-run state"
---

`{{< input type="animate" >}}` is a play / pause / step / reset control over a monotonic
tick. It publishes a plain number through the same reactive node a slider does, so a cell
advances a frame by listing it in `//| input:`.

{{< input name="t" type="animate" min="0" max="60" step="1" fps="10" label="frame" >}}

Each frame is **one** scheduled downstream pass, and the next frame is not requested until
that pass has finished — a slow cell slows the animation rather than queueing behind it.

```{js}
//| name: wave
//| input: t
const k = tali.value("t");
return num.linspace(0, 6.28, 120).map((x) => Math.sin(x + k * 0.12));
```

```{js}
//| label: fig-wave
//| fig-cap: A travelling wave, advanced one frame per tick.
//| input: wave
return Plot.plot({
  height: 180,
  y: { domain: [-1.1, 1.1] },
  marks: [Plot.line(tali.get("wave"), { x: (_, i) => i, y: (d) => d, stroke: "currentColor" })],
});
```

## Accumulating across frames

A cell body re-runs from scratch on every pass, which is what keeps this a scheduler rather
than a reactive virtual machine. `tali.state` is the blessed exception: a small per-cell
store that survives a scheduled re-run, so an iterative demo accumulates instead of
recomputing from nothing each frame.

```{js}
//| input: t
const seen = tali.state("seen", []);
const k = tali.value("t");
if (seen[seen.length - 1] !== k) seen.push(k);
tali.setState("seen", seen.slice(-8));
const p = document.createElement("p");
p.textContent = "last frames: " + tali.state("seen").join(", ");
return p;
```

The store is **per cell**, writing it schedules nothing, and its lifetime is the cell's:
editing this cell mints a new block id and the old store is dropped with the old cell. To
share a value *between* cells, publish it with `//| name:` — an edge the graph can see and
order — rather than writing it here.
